syntax:
Move constructors and move assignment operators are declared with rvalue references as parameters:

```c++
class MyClass {
public:
  MyClass(MyClass&& other) noexcept : data_(std::move(other.data_)) { other.data_ = nullptr; }
  MyClass& operator=(MyClass&& other) noexcept {
    if (this != &other) {
      delete[] data_;
      data_ = std::move(other.data_);
      other.data_ = nullptr;
    }
    return *this;
  }
private:
  int* data_;
};
```

**Practical Usage Notes:**
* `std::move()` is used to explicitly cast an lvalue to an rvalue, allowing the move constructor/assignment operator to be called.
* `noexcept` is usually added to move operations to indicate they won't throw exceptions, potentially enabling further optimizations.
* Always ensure proper resource management in move operations, preventing resource leaks (as shown in the example by setting `other.data_ = nullptr`).
* Avoid unnecessary moves; prefer move-only types when appropriate (using `std::unique_ptr`, `std::shared_ptr`, etc.).
